[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router16
diagnosis_text:
The suspected failure between mswitch0 and router16 is not present. Both physical links in the bundle are healthy:
- mswitch0 bond0 slaves: eth0 (r16_ms0) and eth1 (r16_ms0_2) show MII Status: up at 10 Gbps, Link Failure Count: 0 (cat /proc/net/bonding/bond0). ethtool reports “Link detected: yes” on both.
- router16 bond0 slaves: eth0 and eth1 also show MII Status: up at 10 Gbps (cat /proc/net/bonding/bond0), with ethtool “Link detected: yes.”
- L3 across the link works: mswitch0 10.2.2.2 <-> router16 10.2.2.1 ping bi-directionally with 0% loss, and ARP is resolved.

The real issue is routing on router16: its OSPF daemon is not running (“ospfd is not running” on router16). mswitch0 is acting as the L3 gateway for Campus 1 VLANs (SVIs 172.16.10/20/30.254) and advertising OSPF, but router16 is neither forming an OSPF adjacency on 10.2.2.0/24 nor learning/advertising routes. Mechanism: Campus 1 hosts reach mswitch0 and each other (intra-campus OK), but traffic to Campus 2/backbone forwards toward router16 and fails to return because router16 lacks routes to 172.16.10/20/30. This matches the reported symptoms after maintenance.

Root cause: FRR OSPF service on router16 is down (or its OSPF config was lost) after the maintenance window. Corrective fix: start/enable FRR/ospfd on router16 and restore OSPF configuration to include 10.2.2.0/24 and 172.30.1.0/24; as a temporary workaround, add static routes for 172.16.10/20/30.0/24 via 10.2.2.2 on router16. No physical interface on the mswitch0↔router16 link is down.
=== END ===